FT                   /note="Domain:cl28411:COG2932 Phage repressor protein C,
FT                   contains Cro/C1-type HTH and peptisase s24 domains
FT                   [Mobilome: prophages, transposons]. LexA acts as a
FT                   homodimer to repress a number of genes involved in the
FT                   response to DNA damage (SOS response), including itself and
FT                   RecA. RecA, in the presence of single-stranded DNA, acts as
FT                   a co-protease to activate a latent autolytic protease
FT                   activity (EC 3.4.21.88) of LexA, where the active site Ser
FT                   is part of LexA. The autolytic cleavage site is an Ala-Gly
FT                   bond in LexA (at position 84-85 in E. coli LexA; this
FT                   sequence is replaced by Gly-Gly in Synechocystis). The
FT                   cleavage leads to derepression of the SOS regulon and
FT                   eventually to DNA repair. LexA in Bacillus subtilis is
FT                   called DinR. LexA is much less broadly distributed than
FT                   RecA. [DNA metabolism, DNA replication, recombination, and
FT                   repair, Regulatory functions, DNA interactions]
FT                   [Superfamily, Aligned:89-211, Eval:1.4e-33, score:116.6,
FT                   N-term missing]"
