FT                   /note="Domain:cl27719:Biotin_carb_N Biotin carboxylase,
FT                   N-terminal domain. Carbamoyl-phosphate synthase (CPSase)
FT                   catalyzes the first committed step in pyrimidine, arginine,
FT                   and urea biosynthesis. In general, it is a
FT                   glutamine-dependent enzyme, EC 6.3.5.5, termed CPSase II in
FT                   eukaryotes. An exception is the mammalian mitochondrial
FT                   urea-cycle form, CPSase I, in which the glutamine
FT                   amidotransferase domain active site Cys on the small
FT                   subunit has been lost, and the enzyme is ammonia-dependent.
FT                   In both CPSase I and the closely related,
FT                   glutamine-dependent CPSase III (allosterically activated by
FT                   acetyl-glutamate) demonstrated in some other vertebrates,
FT                   the small and large chain regions are fused in a single
FT                   polypeptide chain. This model represents the large chain of
FT                   glutamine-hydrolysing carbamoyl-phosphate synthases, or the
FT                   corresponding regions of larger, multifunctional proteins,
FT                   as found in all domains of life, and CPSase I forms are
FT                   considered exceptions within the family. In several
FT                   thermophilic species (Methanobacterium thermoautotrophicum,
FT                   Methanococcus jannaschii, Aquifex aeolicus), the large
FT                   subunit appears split, at different points, into two
FT                   separate genes. [Purines, pyrimidines, nucleosides, and
FT                   nucleotides, Pyrimidine ribonucleotide biosynthesis]
FT                   [Superfamily, Aligned:102-316, Eval:1.4e-10, score:59.6,
FT                   C-term missing]"
